{
  "title": "Regression and prediction",
  "kicker": "ENCT 202 · Foundation of Data Science · Chapter 5",
  "subtitle": "Fit a line, read its slope, predict, and see how one outlier can betray it.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "idea",
      "locator": "5.1",
      "title": "Predicting a number",
      "eyebrow": "Regression",
      "body": "<p>Regression predicts a continuous target y from features x. Simple linear regression: ŷ = a + bx. The slope b is “change in y per unit x”; the intercept a is y at x = 0. Classification, by contrast, predicts a category.</p>",
      "questions": [
        {
          "id": "rc",
          "prompt": "Predicting house price is…",
          "choices": [
            {
              "text": "Classification."
            },
            {
              "text": "Regression.",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text": "Clustering."
            }
          ],
          "responses": [
            {
              "title": "A tempting idea.",
              "body": "That predicts a label."
            },
            {
              "title": "Correct.",
              "body": "The target is continuous."
            },
            {
              "title": "It looks right at first.",
              "body": "No target at all."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "fit",
      "locator": "5.2",
      "title": "Least squares",
      "eyebrow": "The best line",
      "body": "<p>Choose a, b minimising the sum of squared residuals. For the sample data slope = <b>0.9</b>, intercept = <b>1.3</b>, R² = <b>0.81</b>.</p>",
      "worked": [
        {
          "toc": "Slope",
          "why": "Slope b = Sxy / Sxx = 9 / 10.",
          "math": "b=0.9"
        },
        {
          "toc": "Intercept",
          "why": "Intercept a = ȳ − b x̄ = 4 − 0.9·3.",
          "math": "a=1.3",
          "hero": true
        }
      ],
      "questions": [
        {
          "id": "res",
          "prompt": "A residual is…",
          "choices": [
            {
              "text": "x minus mean."
            },
            {
              "text": "Actual y minus predicted ŷ.",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text": "Predicted minus mean."
            }
          ],
          "responses": [
            {
              "title": "A tempting idea.",
              "body": "That is a deviation."
            },
            {
              "title": "Correct.",
              "body": "The vertical miss."
            },
            {
              "title": "It looks right at first.",
              "body": "Not the error."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "figures": [
        {
          "id": "lab1",
          "caption": "Figure 5.2.1 — fit",
          "note": "Dashed gaps are residuals.",
          "script": "var lab = create(\"steplab\", {\"name\":\"fit\",\"engine\":\"regression\",\"width\":600,\"height\":440});",
          "expect": {
            "fit": {
              "slope": "0.9",
              "intercept": "1.3",
              "r2": "0.81"
            }
          }
        }
      ],
      "problems": [
        {
          "id": "p-fit",
          "prompt": "<p>With ŷ = 1.3 + 0.9x, predict y at x = 4 and find the residual if the actual is 4.</p>",
          "answer": "ŷ = 4.9; residual = 4 − 4.9 = <b>−0.9</b>."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "outl",
      "locator": "5.3",
      "title": "Outliers can steer the line",
      "eyebrow": "Sensitivity",
      "body": "<p>Replace the last point (5, 6) with (5, 30): slope jumps to <b>5.7</b> and R² falls to <b>0.5732</b>. Squaring makes big errors dominate; robust methods or removing a verified error is the remedy.</p>",
      "questions": [
        {
          "id": "ls",
          "prompt": "Why is least squares sensitive to outliers?",
          "choices": [
            {
              "text": "It ignores extreme points."
            },
            {
              "text": "It uses medians."
            },
            {
              "text": "Errors are squared, so large ones dominate.",
              "correct": true
            }
          ],
          "responses": [
            {
              "title": "A tempting idea.",
              "body": "The opposite."
            },
            {
              "title": "Close, but not it.",
              "body": "It uses means."
            },
            {
              "title": "Correct.",
              "body": "A distant point pulls hard."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "figures": [
        {
          "id": "lab2",
          "caption": "Figure 5.3.1 — with outlier",
          "note": "One far point tilts the whole line.",
          "script": "var lab = create(\"steplab\", {\"name\":\"outl\",\"engine\":\"regression\",\"points\":\"1,2;2,3;3,5;4,4;5,30\",\"width\":600,\"height\":440});",
          "expect": {
            "outl": {
              "slope": "5.7",
              "r2": "0.5732"
            }
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "metrics",
      "locator": "5.4",
      "title": "Measuring error",
      "eyebrow": "How wrong is it?",
      "body": "<p>MAE = mean |error|; MSE = mean error²; RMSE = √MSE (same units as y); R² = 1 − SSres/SStot. RMSE punishes large errors more than MAE. Always compare to a baseline: predicting the mean has R² = 0.</p>",
      "worked": [
        {
          "toc": "Errors",
          "why": "Errors: 1, −2, 3."
        },
        {
          "toc": "RMSE",
          "why": "MSE = (1 + 4 + 9)/3 = 4.67, so RMSE = 2.16 while MAE = 2.",
          "math": "RMSE=2.16",
          "hero": true
        }
      ],
      "questions": [
        {
          "id": "rm",
          "prompt": "RMSE is preferred over MSE for reporting because it is…",
          "choices": [
            {
              "text": "Always smaller."
            },
            {
              "text": "Immune to outliers."
            },
            {
              "text": "In the same units as y.",
              "correct": true
            }
          ],
          "responses": [
            {
              "title": "A tempting idea.",
              "body": "Not always."
            },
            {
              "title": "Close, but not it.",
              "body": "It is not."
            },
            {
              "title": "Correct.",
              "body": "Easier to interpret."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "multi",
      "locator": "5.5",
      "title": "Many features and overfitting",
      "eyebrow": "More is not always better",
      "body": "<p>ŷ = b₀ + b₁x₁ + … + b_k x_k. Adding features always lowers training error, but a model that memorises noise (<em class=\"term\">overfits</em>) does worse on new data. Regularisation (ridge/lasso) adds a penalty on large coefficients; validation (next chapter) detects the problem.</p>",
      "questions": [
        {
          "id": "ov",
          "prompt": "Training error tiny, test error large suggests…",
          "choices": [
            {
              "text": "Underfitting."
            },
            {
              "text": "A perfect model."
            },
            {
              "text": "Overfitting.",
              "correct": true
            }
          ],
          "responses": [
            {
              "title": "A tempting idea.",
              "body": "That has high error on both."
            },
            {
              "title": "Close, but not it.",
              "body": "It fails on new data."
            },
            {
              "title": "Correct.",
              "body": "The model memorised noise."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "logi",
      "locator": "5.6",
      "title": "From regression to classification",
      "eyebrow": "Logistic regression",
      "body": "<p>To predict a yes/no outcome, pass the linear score through the sigmoid σ(s) = 1/(1 + e⁻ˢ) to get a probability in (0, 1), then threshold at 0.5. It is fitted by gradient descent, exactly as in chapter 2.</p>",
      "questions": [
        {
          "id": "sig",
          "prompt": "σ(0) =",
          "choices": [
            {
              "text": "0.5.",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text": "0."
            },
            {
              "text": "1."
            }
          ],
          "responses": [
            {
              "title": "Correct.",
              "body": "1/(1 + 1)."
            },
            {
              "title": "Close, but not it.",
              "body": "That is the limit at −∞."
            },
            {
              "title": "It looks right at first.",
              "body": "That is the limit at +∞."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
